book_context:
The book is a sequence of failed or accidental 'educations,' each measured against what the twentieth century would demand. Chapters I–IV set up the core oppositions. Quincy against Boston, summer against winter, liberty against law, the eighteenth century against the coming age, unity against multiplicity, and Adams against State Street all recur, culminating in the closing dynamic theory of history. Recurring motifs seeded here include:
- silence and holding one's tongue;
- the Cunard steamers as signs of the new world and of escape;
- Sumner as idol, whose later loss teaches that 'a friend in power is a friend lost';
- the father's perfect poise, tested in London;
- the four tools, above all mathematics, that he never mastered;
- the vanished religious instinct;
- the formula 'Education had not begun.'
Skipped chapters (Chicago, Silence, Free Fight, President Grant) cannot carry payoffs, so the setups here must be sufficient on their own.
